        "content": "{\n  \"rule\": \"Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.\",\n  \"text\": \"The last customer brought a toaster wrapped in a towel.\\n\\nWe had already unplugged the lamps. Dad was taking the brass letters off the shop door, working carefully with a screwdriver too small for the screws.\\n\\n“Closing down,” I told her.\\n\\n“I know.” She set the toaster on the counter. “Your father fixed it in 1987.”\\n\\nDad put down the screwdriver.\\n\\nThe woman unfolded the towel. One lever was missing its black cap. There was a dent along the side, neatly avoiding the faded inspection sticker.\\n\\n“Still heats,” she said. “Won’t stay down.”\\n\\nI looked at the boxes behind us. Forty-three years of spare parts, sorted into things worth selling and things nobody wanted.\\n\\nDad pressed the bare lever with his thumb.\\n\\n“Come back Thursday.”\\n\\n“We hand over the keys tomorrow,” I said.\\n\\nHe pressed it again.\\n\\nThe woman began wrapping the toaster.\\n\\n“Never mind. You’ve earned a rest.”\\n\\nDad held the towel still.\\n\\n“Leave it with me.”\\n\\nAfter she went, he carried it to the only bench we hadn’t cleared. I found the extension lead while he fetched his glasses from the empty drawer where he always kept them.\\n\\nFor a while I swept around his shoes.\\n\\nThen he asked for a small spring.\\n\\n“Which box?”\\n\\n“The biscuit tin.”\\n\\nThere were six biscuit tins. I opened them one by one.\\n\\nOutside, the signwriter arrived to measure the window. I let him in and moved our boxes out of his way.\\n\\nDad fitted the spring and lowered the lever. This time it stayed.\\n\\n“Bread?” he asked.\\n\\nI went to the corner shop and bought a loaf, butter, and two paper plates.\"\n}",
